---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import ArticleFaq from '../../../components/ArticleFaq.astro';
import CTASection from '../../../components/CTASection.astro';
import { MANUFACTURER, TRADE_TERMS, RELATIONSHIP } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'OEM Battery MOQ & Lead Time Explained | Dingwei Battery';
const description = 'What MOQ and lead time mean for OEM battery orders, and how they are set — with Chengguang Energy’s reference terms.';
const articleTitle = 'OEM battery MOQ & lead time';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Why is battery MOQ tied to a container?', a: 'Batteries are heavy, so a 20ft container hits its weight limit before its volume limit. MOQ follows the container naturally, and mixing models fills it efficiently.' },
  { q: 'Can I get a lower MOQ for a first order?', a: 'Sometimes — discuss a prototype or pilot batch before committing to the full production MOQ. Expect a higher unit cost at low volume.' },
  { q: 'Does lead-acid production have a seasonal cycle?', a: 'No. Unlike agricultural products, lead-acid batteries are produced year-round, so lead time is stable rather than seasonal.' },
];
---
<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle} articleSection="Logistics" dateModified="2026-09-03" faqs={faqs}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge</span>
    <h1>OEM battery MOQ &amp; lead time</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-01 · Source: Chengguang Energy technical documentation</p>
    <p class="lead">MOQ and lead time are set by production economics and container logistics, not by negotiation alone. Here is how they work.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> OEM battery MOQ follows the container because batteries are heavy — a 20ft container hits its weight limit before its volume limit, so MOQ is tied to weight, not space. Mixing models into one container keeps unit cost down, and lead time is stable year-round because lead-acid has no seasonal cycle.
    </div>

    <h2>What drives MOQ</h2>
    <p>Battery MOQ is usually tied to a container load, because shipping cost per unit collapses at FCL. {MANUFACTURER.name}&rsquo;s reference MOQ is {TRADE_TERMS.moq}.</p>

    <h2>What drives lead time</h2>
    <p>Lead time covers order confirmation, production and pre-shipment testing. {MANUFACTURER.name}&rsquo;s reference lead time is {TRADE_TERMS.leadTime}; custom-label orders add sampling time.</p>

    <h2>How to reduce both</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Order in full container multiples</li>
      <li>Consolidate mixed models into one container</li>
      <li>Confirm specification early to avoid sampling delays</li>
      <li>Plan around the harvest-free, year-round production of lead-acid batteries</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/contact/">Confirm MOQ and lead time &rarr;</a> · <a href="/knowledge/how-oem-battery-programs-work/">How OEM programs run &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2>Container economics in one line</h2>
    <p>Unit cost drops when you fill a 20ft container because the fixed export and freight overhead is spread across more batteries. Once the container is full, adding more models to that same load usually keeps unit cost lower than splitting into part loads.</p>
    <p>Send one complete volume and model plan in your <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-rfq-guide/">OEM battery RFQ</a> so the manufacturer can build the container efficiently.</p>
    <p>{RELATIONSHIP}</p>

    <h2>Lead-acid is heavy: the container math</h2>
    <p>Lead-acid batteries are dense, so a 20ft container usually reaches its weight limit before its volume limit. MOQ therefore follows the container rather than a marketing number. Mixing several models into one FCL spreads that weight and freight across more line items, which usually keeps unit cost lower than splitting part loads.</p>
    <p>{MANUFACTURER.name}&rsquo;s reference MOQ of {TRADE_TERMS.moq} is a container-logic term, not a per-model minimum. Send the full model mix in the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-rfq-guide/">OEM battery RFQ</a> so the load can be planned correctly.</p>

    <h2>Why lead-acid has no seasonal cycle</h2>
    <p>Lead-acid production is not tied to a harvest or seasonal raw-material window. The manufacturing line can run through the year, which keeps lead time more stable than seasonal categories. For an importer, that means a confirmed production lead time is a planning variable, not a harvest risk.</p>
    <p>Use that stability when you set the volume plan in the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-rfq-guide/">OEM battery RFQ</a> or walk through the <a href="/knowledge/how-oem-battery-programs-work/">OEM program phases</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <ArticleFaq faqs={faqs} />

  <CTASection title="Confirm MOQ and lead time for your order" />
</ArticleLayout>
